    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and it passes. \n\n## Summary\n\nThe change is implemented and the test suite is green.\n\n**What I did** \u2014 in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`, `generateCustomerCode(...)` previously returned the raw `<REGION>-<HASH8>` base code with no collision handling. I:\n\n1. Kept the base code computation (`region + \"-\" + hash8`) and routed it through a new `deduplicateCustomerCode(baseCode)` helper.\n2. `deduplicateCustomerCode` gathers every existing owner's `customerCode` into a set. If the base code is unused, it returns it unchanged. Otherwise it appends `-<n>`, starting at `n = 2` and incrementing to the smallest `n \u2265 2` that yields a code no existing owner holds, then returns the de-duplicated code.\n\nThis runs before `generateMembershipNumber`, so the membership number is derived from the already-deduplicated customer code.\n\n**Verification** \u2014 `./mvnw -o test` builds successfully; `AcceptanceTest` (the sole test class, including the new `coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` guarantee that distinct owners always get distinct customer codes) passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\nOne note on scope: as the test comment itself acknowledges, a genuine hash collision can't be forced black-box because telephone uniqueness already blocks identical `normalizedTelephone + lastName`. The de-duplication logic is nonetheless implemented per the spec so that if two owners ever did resolve to the same base code, the second would receive `<base>-2`, the third `<base>-3`, and so on.",
